t much harder to compress efficiently. ### 4. Higher target bitrate to preserve detail - YouTube 1080p typically streams at **3 – 8 Mbps** (megabits per second). That’s ~1.3 – 3.5 GB per hour. - Stadia’s “Best” 1080p setting officially recommended **at least 10 Mbps**, but in practice used **15 – 20 Mbps or more** continuously. At 20 Mbps you’re consuming about **9 GB per hour**, roughly 3× to 7× a YouTube video of the same resolution. ### 5. Extra data for error correction & audio - Because Stadia can’t retransmit lost packets (that would add delay), it often adds **Forward Error Correction (FEC)** data—extra bits that let the receiver rebuild lost packets without asking for a resend. This adds ~5–10% overhead. - The audio is uncompressed or lightly compressed to keep it in sync with the ultra‑low‑latency video. ### Quick comparison (typical figures) | Service | Resolution | Approx. Bitrate | Data per hour | |-----------------|--------------|-----------------|---------------| | YouTube (VP9) | 1080p | 3–8 Mbps | ~1.3–3.6 GB | | Stadia (1080p) | 1080p | 15–20 Mbps | ~6.8–9 GB | So the answer boils down to: **game streaming trades compression efficiency for real‑time speed, constant high bitrate, and no buffering, all of which demand far more data even at the same nominal resolution.**
